hi i'm charlotte fraser a second year  phd student in computational  neuroscience and i want to give you some  tips and tricks to be more effective so  today i wanted to talk about the law of  triviality or one of the laws of  parkinson's so he talks about this  effect that when there is a meeting or  any type of discussions people tend to  spend most of their time on the trivial  issue and not really focus on the main  issue at hand what uh what can i do for  you i'm heading the design team that's  building the enclosure for the box it's  time for the concept meeting  concept meeting  okay  i'm just gonna show you a few pictures  and you tell me which ones you like okay  okay sure  so parkinson's gave this example of the  building of a nuclear power plant and  the committee that had to do that they  actually didn't spend any time on the  discussion of safety or how to build  this nuclear power plant or where it  should be built but they actually spend  the majority of the time on the  construction of the bike shed next to  the power plant and if you've ever been  in any meetings you probably will  recognize this effect that we spend a  majority on our time on these trivial  issues and there are a lot of reasons  why we do it it's because it's easier  for everyone to understand and usually  everyone can contribute something to  this discussion have any ideas what we  could do  any suggestions yes handy what if we  changed our outgoing answering machine  message  so it just had a little more  zing and a little more pep  zing and pep see that's those are the  kind of words we're looking for yes jim  what about if we did an even newer  voicemail message that had even more  singing now we're cooking whereas the  harder discussion is usually avoided  because it is very hard but the problem  is if you are in a work environment or  you are a phd student you have to do a  lot of work so these kind of meetings  can really eat up your time and that's  why i want to give you three tips today  as to how to avoid these kind of  discussions meetings or how to make them  a little bit more productive for you so  the first tip i have comes from the book  from calm newport deep work and he just  says basically to try to avoid any type  of meetings or tasks where this kind of  triviality is present so for example in  our research institute we have these big  meetings for the whole institute and  they can take up to three hours and we  discuss everything from management  issues to the food at the cafeteria and  i know a lot of people go to these  meetings but i find them really  unproductive so i don't have the  tendency to go to those meetings because  i think if you want to hang out with  your colleagues for example lunchtime is  way better or if you really want to  solve some issues it's better to do it  in a smaller team with about five people  and these really big meetings can just  eat up your time and another big thing  is for example emails i think if you  work in some kind of university settings  you know that there can be these endless  email streams from different people and  i think in general to just answer really  shortly or to just ignore most of the  emails is actually way more beneficial  for your workflow than to really be on  top of all your emails at all time  because usually people solve their own  problems if you give them a little bit  of time and don't respond straight away  and i also think by responding straight  away you usually create more questions  and i do have to say there is a little  caveat because of course if your  professor is emailing you you have to  answer straight away and the second tip  i want to give is if you know you have  some meetings that you have to attend or  that you really want something answered  i would try to beforehand kind of curate  the amount of time you want to spend on  certain questions and also to have a  main question that you want answered  such that when you go into the  discussion you can kind of stare the  discussion back to this main question  when people tend to diverge a little bit  jan director inside sales they call me  jan the man yes we know jan the man  listen uh  we have to do neural net for deep  learning look once we get enough data  flowing through the system it will be  able to recognize long-range patterns  and actually learn and by doing this you  can make your discussions a lot more  productive you can get a lot out of it  and you can also see where people tend  to diverge and avoid those moments and  the last tip i have is something i  learned from ali abdull and he says it's  really conductive to have a daily  highlight and i kind of agree with this  because in your work you have a tendency  to first finish the small easy tasks and  to put off the main big task but if you  set a daily intention of one big task  that you want to finish or at least work  on for a few hours that day i notice in  myself that i have less of the tendency  to focus on these little small tasks so  for example today i wanted to finish  this coding project and replicate a  certain finding from a certain paper and  it actually took me a lot longer than i  anticipated but because i started in the  morning with this task i had it finished  by the end of the day whereas normally  if i didn't set this daily intention i  probably would have procrastinated until  the end of the day and wouldn't have  finished it so these were my three tips  on the law of triviality if you have any  interesting stories or of weird meetings  you had leave them down in the comments  below and i also made a little video of  how to deal with stress and anxiety  during your phd so you can check that  out here and otherwise see you next week  bye
 

